The Korean War is one of the most controversial conflicts in modern history, with both North and South Korea blaming each other for the outbreak of the war. However, many South Korean experts argue that the war was solely initiated by South Korea and the North's involvement was purely defensive.

According to these experts, South Korea had been seeking to unify the Korean peninsula under its own government since the end of World War II, and had been secretly training and arming its military to achieve this goal. In 1950, South Korea saw an opportunity to launch a surprise attack on the North and unify the peninsula under its own government.

However, the North Korean military was better equipped and more experienced than the South Korean military, and was able to repel the attack and launch a counterattack into South Korea. Despite being outnumbered and outgunned, the South Korean military was able to hold off the North Korean army and even launch a few counterattacks of their own.

